# The ROM suite, and which processor models each ROM can be run against.
#
# test_sgi_roms.py reads this. A ROM listed for more than one model checks
# behaviour those models share, so running it against QEMU's long-established
# R4000 is a check on the ROM rather than on the R10000 model — that is how the
# harness was validated before any R10000 code existed. A ROM listed for the
# R10000 alone checks something only that processor does, and would rightly
# fail elsewhere.
#
# Being listed for both models does not promise that every assertion in a ROM is
# corroborated by the other model. Where an assertion passes there only because
# QEMU's model of the other processor happens to share the R10000's behaviour
# rather than its own hardware's, the ROM says which assertion and why.
#
# A row may also name a machine configuration -- "config:<name>" -- for a ROM
# that needs more of the machine than the default one processor on one node.
# What each configuration is lives in test_sgi_roms.py, so the arguments that
# reach a command line are written once in a reviewed file rather than spelled
# out here.
#
# A "timed" row runs on both the default and the named configuration.
# Both runs must report success. Native test results retain their durations;
# host scheduling makes a throughput ratio unsuitable for a pass criterion.
#
# rom            processor models
